# selinux — real-world examples

SELinux management commands. (Note: OpenSUSE Tumbleweed defaults to AppArmor;
SELinux is standard on RHEL/Fedora. Equivalents noted at the bottom.)

## View context

```sh
# -Z shows the SELinux context (user:role:type:level)
ls -alZ
ps -eZ | grep httpd
id -Z
```

## Change a file's context

```sh
# chcon changes context on files/directories (temporary — reset by relabel)
chcon -R -t usr_t directory1/     # recursively set type usr_t

# Persistent: define the rule, then apply it
semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t "/srv/www(/.*)?"
restorecon -Rv /srv/www           # relabel to match policy
```

## Modes: permissive vs enforcing

```sh
getenforce                # show current mode
setenforce 0              # switch to Permissive (log, don't block)
setenforce 1              # switch to Enforcing

# Persist across reboots
sudo vim /etc/selinux/config      # set SELINUX=enforcing|permissive|disabled
```

## Booleans and ports

```sh
# List booleans
getsebool -a | grep httpd

# Flip a boolean persistently (-P)
setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect on

# Let a service listen on a nonstandard port
semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8080
```

## Troubleshooting denials

```sh
# Recent denials
ausearch -m avc -ts recent

# Human-readable explanations + suggested fixes
sealert -a /var/log/audit/audit.log
```

## Homelab note (Tumbleweed uses AppArmor)

```sh
aa-status                          # AppArmor equivalent of getenforce/sestatus
sudo aa-complain /usr/sbin/smbd    # like permissive for one profile
sudo aa-enforce  /usr/sbin/smbd    # like enforcing for one profile
```

## Flag reference

| Command | Meaning |
|---------|---------|
| `ls -Z`, `ps -Z` | Show SELinux context |
| `chcon -t <type>` | Temporary context change |
| `restorecon -R` | Reset context from policy |
| `semanage fcontext` | Persistent context rule |
| `getenforce` / `setenforce` | Show / set mode |
| `getsebool` / `setsebool -P` | Read / set booleans |
